    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media, from left‑liberal to conservative, consistently portray the recent spikes in arrivals—particularly on the Canary Islands and at border points—as a pressing strain on housing, health and public‑order capacities that demands a coordinated state response. At the same time, outlets across the spectrum repeatedly link migration to public‑safety debates, foregrounding criminal incidents involving migrants as evidence for tightening asylum procedures and accelerating deportations. Finally, there is a broad consensus that the existing asylum framework is inefficient, prompting calls for clearer legal rules, faster processing and reliable bilateral return agreements to restore procedural predictability.",
      "controversial_points": "The core conflict pits national security concerns against humanitarian obligations. Right-wing outlets link migration to crime and cultural erosion, while left-leaning media stress integration and anti‑racist narratives. The debate over legal status divides parties between restrictive asylum reforms and calls for guaranteed work‑based residency. Media framing of border incidents creates a tension between sovereignty demands and EU solidarity responsibilities."
